                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.506/16: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Collier</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-09-21T15:00:00-04:00"><hi rendition="#italic">September 21,
                                1927—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 7:40 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>138. Regular session of Congress has terminated. Government contemplates calling
                    special session about October 6th. Insurance bill will then come before Senate.
                    I request Department’s consideration of the fact that the tax on policies
                    negotiated by foreigners with foreign companies and executed in foreign
                    countries affects not only insurance on real estate, that is, buildings, but
                    also insurance on personal property, machinery and other chattels and also
                    insurance against loss of prospective profits caused by interruption of business
                    due to fire. Am informed that one of three great American copper companies
                    carries 12 million dollars against loss of prospective profits, presumably the
                    others carry proportionate insurance. Attorneys for American interests have
                    argued that if such contracts relating to personal property even though used in
                    connection with or derived from real estate situated in Chile can thus be taxed,
                    it would appear that any contract affecting such personal property, as for
                    instance mortgages and assignments, could be taxed. Does this in the
                    Department’s opinion affect its decision that no representation should be made
                    against this feature of the law?</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Collier</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
